\title{Three-Sample Tropical Training as Median-Seeking\\Piecewise-Linear Gradient Flow}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 30, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze a scalar tropical neuron trained on three observations under absolute-error loss. After absorbing fixed feature contributions into the labels, the problem is governed by ordered reduced targets $a\le m\le c$ and the objective
\[
L(x)=|x-a|+|x-m|+|x-c|.
\]
The middle target $m$ is the unique global minimizer. We study the clipped unit-speed piecewise-linear flow
\[
\Phi_t(x)=
\begin{cases}
\min\{m,x+t\},&x<m,\\
\max\{m,x-t\},&x\ge m,
\end{cases}
\]
a canonical median-seeking subgradient dynamics. Every initialization $x_0$ reaches $m$ after time $|x_0-m|$ and remains there, so convergence is finite-time rather than merely asymptotic. A point minimizes empirical loss if and only if it is fixed by every positive-time flow map. Thus statistical optimality, dynamical stationarity, and the median condition coincide. We give proof sketches, algorithms, examples, and extensions to weighted samples, projective tropical spaces, smooth approximations, multiple neurons, and discrete descent.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Tropical models replace smooth algebraic combinations by max-affine or min-affine operations. Their parameter spaces are divided into polyhedral regions on which outputs and losses have affine descriptions. This geometry is particularly relevant when a smooth architecture is examined in a large-scale or low-temperature limit: log-sum-exp expressions sharpen toward maxima, and smoothly varying gradients approach piecewise-constant subgradients.

Nonsmoothness can complicate conventional differential analysis, but it may also expose exact dynamics that smooth models conceal. This paper develops the smallest nontrivial example. A one-parameter tropical neuron is trained on three reduced scalar observations using an $L^1$ objective. The loss is the sum of three absolute residuals, and the dynamics move directly toward their median.

Three features make the example useful. First, the optimizer is an order statistic rather than an average, connecting tropical learning to robust estimation. Second, the loss is convex but not differentiable at its data knots, so a piecewise-linear subgradient description is natural. Third, the proposed flow reaches equilibrium in an explicitly bounded finite time. The model distinguishes ordinary limit convergence from eventual exact stationarity.

Our main connector theorem states that global empirical-risk minimization is equivalent to invariance under every positive-time flow map; both conditions hold precisely at the median. Every initial condition converges globally and becomes equal to the median once elapsed time covers the initial distance.

The analysis concerns the reduced scalar tropical model itself. It does not establish convergence from a finite-temperature smooth network to this limit, nor does it address arbitrary multidimensional architectures. Those are natural extensions discussed below.

\section{Reduced three-sample model}

\subsection{Tropical reduction}

Consider a scalar projective coordinate $x$ for a tropical neuron. For each observation, subtract the fixed tropical feature contribution from its label. This produces a reduced target against which $x$ is compared directly. Sort the three reduced targets as
\[
a\le m\le c.
\]
No strict inequality is required. Repeated targets are permitted, and $m$ is the median even when $a=m$ or $m=c$.

\begin{definition}[Three-point tropical absolute loss]
For ordered reduced targets $a\le m\le c$ and $x\in\mathbb R$, define
\[
L_{a,m,c}(x)=|x-a|+|x-m|+|x-c|.
\]
\end{definition}

The objective is continuous, convex, coercive, and piecewise affine, with possible breakpoints at $a,m,c$. Away from repeated knots its slopes are
\[
-3\quad(x<a),\qquad -1\quad(a<x<m),\qquad
1\quad(m<x<c),\qquad 3\quad(x>c).
\]
Thus the sign changes at $m$. The arguments below avoid differentiability and remain valid when targets coincide.

\begin{definition}[Clipped unit-speed tropical flow]
For $t\ge0$, median $m$, and initial point $x\in\mathbb R$, define
\[
\Phi_t^m(x)=
\begin{cases}
\min\{m,x+t\},&x<m,\\
\max\{m,x-t\},&x\ge m.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

The trajectory moves toward $m$ at unit speed and clips there to prevent overshoot. Although the raw subgradient magnitude of $L_{a,m,c}$ varies by region, this normalized flow follows the loss-decreasing direction and retains only its orientation. It is therefore a normalized subgradient flow or median-seeking tropical training dynamics. Equivalently,
\[
\Phi_t^m(x)=m+\sgn(x-m)\max\{|x-m|-t,0\},
\]
where $\sgn(0)=0$, and hence
\[
|\Phi_t^m(x)-m|=\max\{|x-m|-t,0\}.
\]

\section{Geometry of the loss}

\begin{lemma}[Outer-distance bound]
For every $x\in\mathbb R$ and $a\le c$,
\[
|x-a|+|x-c|\ge c-a.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The triangle inequality gives
\[
|c-a|=|(x-a)-(x-c)|\le |x-a|+|x-c|.
\]
Since $c-a\ge0$, $|c-a|=c-a$. At each $x\in[a,c]$, equality holds because the two distances partition the interval.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Median minimization]
Let $a\le m\le c$. For every $x\in\mathbb R$,
\[
L_{a,m,c}(m)\le L_{a,m,c}(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At the median,
\[
L_{a,m,c}(m)=|m-a|+0+|m-c|=(m-a)+(c-m)=c-a.
\]
For arbitrary $x$, the outer-distance bound and nonnegativity give
\[
L_{a,m,c}(x)=|x-a|+|x-c|+|x-m|\ge(c-a)+0=L_{a,m,c}(m).
\]
\end{proof}

The preceding argument establishes minimality but not uniqueness by itself, because the outer-distance inequality is an equality throughout $[a,c]$. The middle residual supplies strictness.

\begin{lemma}[Strict increase away from the median]
If $a\le m\le c$, then
\[
x<m\Longrightarrow L_{a,m,c}(m)<L_{a,m,c}(x)
\]
and
\[
m<x\Longrightarrow L_{a,m,c}(m)<L_{a,m,c}(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose first that $x<m$. If $a\le x<m$, expansion gives
\[
L_{a,m,c}(x)=(x-a)+(m-x)+(c-x)=m+c-a-x,
\]
so its difference from $L_{a,m,c}(m)=c-a$ is $m-x>0$. If $x<a$, then
\[
L_{a,m,c}(x)=(a-x)+(m-x)+(c-x)
\]
and the difference is $2(a-x)+(m-x)>0$.

The right side is analogous. If $m<x\le c$, the difference is $x-m>0$. If $x>c$, the difference is $2(x-c)+(x-m)>0$. These cases include possible equalities among the targets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unique empirical-risk minimizer]
For $a\le m\le c$ and $x\in\mathbb R$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $L_{a,m,c}(x)\le L_{a,m,c}(y)$ for every $y\in\mathbb R$;
\item $x=m$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Median minimization proves that $m$ satisfies (i). If a minimizing $x$ were smaller or larger than $m$, strict increase away from the median would show that $m$ has lower loss, a contradiction. Trichotomy leaves $x=m$.
\end{proof}

The result remains valid with repeated targets. If $a=m<c$, two observations occur at $m$, and $m$ is uniquely optimal. The same holds when $a<m=c$ or all values coincide.

\section{Dynamics of the clipped flow}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-time arrival]
Let $m,x\in\mathbb R$ and $t\ge0$. If $|x-m|\le t$, then
\[
\Phi_t^m(x)=m.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x<m$, then $m-x=|x-m|\le t$, hence $m\le x+t$ and
$\min\{m,x+t\}=m$. If $x\ge m$, then $x-m=|x-m|\le t$, hence $x-t\le m$ and $\max\{m,x-t\}=m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact hitting time]
For initialization $x_0$, the trajectory reaches $m$ by time
\[
T(x_0)=|x_0-m|.
\]
If $x_0\ne m$, no smaller nonnegative time reaches $m$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Arrival at $T(x_0)$ follows from finite-time arrival. For $0\le t<T(x_0)$, the distance formula leaves positive residual distance $T(x_0)-t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Semigroup law]
For $s,t\ge0$ and $x\in\mathbb R$,
\[
\Phi_s^m(\Phi_t^m(x))=\Phi_{s+t}^m(x).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
After time $t$, distance from $m$ is $\max\{|x-m|-t,0\}$ and the side is unchanged unless arrival has occurred. Another duration $s$ gives
\[
\max\{\max\{|x-m|-t,0\}-s,0\}
=\max\{|x-m|-(s+t),0\}.
\]
The sign is preserved before arrival and zero afterward.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point characterization]
For $m,x\in\mathbb R$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\Phi_t^m(x)=x$ for every $t>0$;
\item $x=m$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x=m$, clipping keeps it fixed. Conversely, assume invariance and choose $t=|x-m|+1$. Finite-time arrival gives $\Phi_t^m(x)=m$, while invariance gives $\Phi_t^m(x)=x$. Hence $x=m$.
\end{proof}

For this dynamics, invariance under any one positive duration already forces $x=m$: a nonmedian point either moves by that positive amount or arrives at the distinct point $m$.

\begin{theorem}[Global finite-time convergence]
For every $m,x_0\in\mathbb R$,
\[
\lim_{t\to\infty}\Phi_t^m(x_0)=m.
\]
More strongly, $\Phi_t^m(x_0)=m$ for every $t\ge|x_0-m|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The stronger statement is finite-time arrival. An eventually constant trajectory converges to its eventual value.
\end{proof}

Many smooth gradient flows approach equilibrium without reaching it in finite time. The nonsmooth normalized dynamics instead give exact arrival.

\section{The optimization--dynamics connector}

\begin{theorem}[Three-sample tropical training connector]
Let $a\le m\le c$. For every $x\in\mathbb R$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $x$ is a global minimizer: $L_{a,m,c}(x)\le L_{a,m,c}(y)$ for every $y\in\mathbb R$;
\item $x$ is dynamically stationary: $\Phi_t^m(x)=x$ for every $t>0$;
\item $x=m$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, every initialization $x_0$ reaches this common point after time $|x_0-m|$ and converges to it as $t\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The unique-minimizer corollary identifies global minimizers exactly with $m$. The fixed-point theorem identifies points fixed by all positive-time maps exactly with $m$. Global finite-time convergence supplies the final assertion.
\end{proof}

The theorem connects order statistics, optimization, and dynamics. It gives uniqueness without strict convexity: although the loss has linear stretches, the odd number of observations makes the median residual enforce a unique optimum.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Three-sample empirical-risk minimization]
Given reduced targets $r_1,r_2,r_3$, sort them as $a\le m\le c$ and return $m$.
\end{algorithm}

A comparison sort uses constant storage and, for three elements, a constant number of comparisons. For variable input size $n$, sorting costs $O(n\log n)$; a selection method can find a median in expected $O(n)$ time. Here $n=3$, so time and space are $O(1)$.

\begin{algorithm}[Exact tropical flow evaluation]
Given median $m$, time $t\ge0$, and initialization $x$, return $\min\{m,x+t\}$ if $x<m$; otherwise return $\max\{m,x-t\}$.
\end{algorithm}

This uses one comparison and constantly many arithmetic operations, hence $O(1)$ time and auxiliary space. Unlike numerical integration, it has no discretization error because it evaluates the trajectory in closed form.

For visualization, choose $0=t_0<t_1<\cdots<t_N$ and evaluate the flow at each time. This takes $O(N)$ time and $O(N)$ space if all samples are retained, or $O(1)$ space for streaming. Including $|x_0-m|$ in the grid displays the transition to stationarity exactly.

\section{Numerical examples}

Take $a=-2$, $m=1$, and $c=5$. At the optimizer,
\[
L(1)=|3|+|0|+|-4|=7.
\]
At two comparison points,
\[
L(-1)=1+2+6=9,
\qquad
L(4)=6+3+1=10.
\]
For $x_0=-2$, the hitting time is $|-2-1|=3$. At times $0,1,2,3,4$, the states are
\[
-2,-1,0,1,1.
\]
For $x_0=5$, the hitting time is $4$, and states at integer times $0$ through $5$ are
\[
5,4,3,2,1,1.
\]
Large times do not cause overshoot; for example, $\Phi_{10}^1(5)=1$.

\section{Structural consequences of the exact formula}

The closed-form distance law yields several useful deductions.

\begin{proposition}[Nonexpansiveness]
For fixed $m$ and $t\ge0$, the map $x\mapsto\Phi_t^m(x)$ is $1$-Lipschitz:
\[
|\Phi_t^m(x)-\Phi_t^m(y)|\le|x-y|
\]
for all $x,y\in\mathbb R$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The flow map is continuous, nondecreasing, and piecewise affine with slopes only $0$ and $1$. On regions where points travel without clipping, separation is preserved; when clipping occurs, separation can only decrease. The Lipschitz bound follows.
\end{proof}

Thus perturbations in initialization are never amplified. Two trajectories may preserve their separation while traveling on the same side, but clipping can only reduce it.

\begin{proposition}[Monotone distance and eventual zero loss gap]
Along every trajectory,
\[
|\Phi_t^m(x_0)-m|=\max\{|x_0-m|-t,0\}.
\]
The empirical loss $L_{a,m,c}(\Phi_t^m(x_0))$ is nonincreasing in $t$ and equals its minimum $c-a$ for every $t\ge|x_0-m|$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The distance identity follows by expanding the two flow branches. Before arrival, the state moves toward $m$ without changing sides, and strict increase of loss away from the median shows that this movement strictly lowers loss. After arrival, state and loss are constant. At $m$, median minimization gives $L_{a,m,c}(m)=c-a$.
\end{proof}

Loss can decrease at different linear rates as the trajectory crosses $a$ or $c$, since the number of residuals pulling in each direction changes. Parameter distance nevertheless falls at unit rate. The normalized flow uses the sign of a descent direction rather than the full subgradient magnitude.

\begin{proposition}[Translation equivariance]
For every common offset $q\in\mathbb R$,
\[
L_{a+q,m+q,c+q}(x+q)=L_{a,m,c}(x)
\]
and
\[
\Phi_t^{m+q}(x+q)=\Phi_t^m(x)+q.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every residual is unchanged because $(x+q)-(a+q)=x-a$, and similarly for $m$ and $c$. Flow-branch inequalities are unchanged, while adding $q$ commutes with the minimum and maximum.
\end{proof}

Translation equivariance explains why subtracting fixed feature contributions is natural: only relative positions matter. It also anticipates the projective viewpoint, in which common translations are redundant descriptions of the same tropical state.

\section{Interpretation and applications}

\subsection{Robustness}
The optimizer depends on ordering, not magnitudes through an average. If $c$ moves farther right while $a\le m\le c$ remains true, the optimizer stays at $m$. This is the elementary source of the median's resistance to one extreme observation. The optimal value does change: $L(m)=c-a$ records the outer span. Thus the estimate is robust even though empirical loss reflects outlier distance.

\subsection{Stopping certificates}
The distance law gives a deterministic certificate. If training begins at $x_0$, then elapsed continuous time $t\ge|x_0-m|$ certifies equality with the optimizer. The idealized model needs no tolerance.

\subsection{Polyhedral optimization}
The scalar example is a prototype of higher-dimensional tropical learning. In larger models, active max-affine terms partition parameter space into polyhedra. Within a cell, residuals and descent directions can be affine or constant; crossing a wall changes the active pattern. The present flow shows how clipping at a nonsmooth optimum turns regional rules into a globally convergent trajectory.

\subsection{Projective interpretation}
Tropical vectors are often considered modulo common translation. A scalar coordinate may be viewed as a chart after redundant translation has been removed. An intrinsic extension requires a projective metric and a proof that the dynamics are independent of representatives. The current model captures the chart-level median mechanism.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The theorem uses a scalar parameter, three unweighted observations, absolute loss, and a normalized clipped flow centered at the median. Each assumption suggests an extension.

A smooth log-sum-exp neuron depending on a scale parameter could be shown to approach the piecewise-linear system. Loss convergence alone is insufficient; trajectory convergence must control behavior near moving nonsmooth boundaries.

The scalar chart should be replaced by a tropical projective torus. Weight vectors would be quotiented by common translation, equipped with a projective metric, and the flow shown invariant under representatives.

Odd finite samples should yield the ordinary median, and positive weights a weighted median. For even samples, the minimizer set is the interval between the center order statistics. Dynamics may possess a continuum of equilibria, with initialization or flow convention selecting among them.

Multiple tropical neurons produce sums and differences of max-affine terms. Their activation patterns form a polyhedral complex. Convex subclasses may admit global convergence; nonconvex cases require local analysis, error bounds, or conditions excluding cycling among regions.

Practical training is discrete. Constant-step subgradient descent can overshoot the median repeatedly. Clipping, proximal updates, or diminishing step sizes may restore convergence. One can quantify how closely an interpolated discrete trajectory shadows the continuous flow.

Perturbation theory should measure how labels or features alter the reduced median, linking training stability to robust statistics. Alternative losses also change estimators and dynamics: squared loss selects the mean and gives smooth contraction, while Huber loss interpolates between mean-like and median-like behavior.

\section{Conclusion}

For three ordered reduced targets $a\le m\le c$, the scalar tropical loss
\[
L(x)=|x-a|+|x-m|+|x-c|
\]
has unique global minimizer $m$. The clipped unit-speed flow moves any initialization toward that point without overshoot and arrives after time $|x_0-m|$. The median is the unique point fixed by every positive-time flow map. Empirical-risk minimization, dynamical stationarity, and the order-statistical median condition are therefore equivalent.

The model captures characteristic features of tropical learning: polyhedral objectives, nonsmooth descent, robust estimators, and finite-time stabilization. It is a base case for studying how smooth optimization degenerates into piecewise-linear tropical dynamics and how the mechanism scales to weighted data, projective spaces, discrete algorithms, and multi-neuron networks.

